+# Receipts stopped
+
+For the volunteer on call when members say their receipts stopped arriving. Brings the worker back and sends the
+receipts that piled up; while it is down, no member gets a receipt for a captured pantry order. Written after the
+September 14 incident, the second time it happened: the first time the one volunteer who knew the fix was asleep and
+members waited until noon. Owner: Tomás.
+
+## Before you begin
+
+- A shell with `sudo` on the server that runs larder, to start the `larder-worker` service.
+- `psql` access to the `larder` database, to count the receipt jobs in the queue.
+- The worker that sends receipts is described in [Architecture](../architecture.md#parts): receipts are jobs in a
+  Postgres queue, sent by `larder/receipts.py`.
+
+## Steps
+
+1. Check the worker: `systemctl status larder-worker`.
+
+   Expected: `inactive (dead)`, usually since just after the last deploy, because the deploy script restarts only the
+   web app. If it says `active (running)`, the worker is not the cause: go to [If it fails](#if-it-fails).
+2. Count the jobs waiting, before restarting anything:
+   `psql larder -c "select status, count(*) from procrastinate_jobs group by status"`.
+
+   Expected: a pile of `todo` jobs, one per receipt not sent (212 on September 14), `0` doing. Note the `todo` and
+   `failed` counts.
+3. Start the worker: `sudo systemctl start larder-worker`.
+
+   Expected: `systemctl status larder-worker` says `active (running)`.
+4. A minute later, run the query from step 2 again, and repeat until `todo` reaches 0.
+
+   Expected: `todo` falls by about 40 a minute and reaches 0; members get their receipts. A few `failed` jobs are
+   receipts to members whose email address bounces: they need no action.
+
+## If it fails
+
+If the worker dies again right after starting, or `todo` does not go down, read its last log lines,
+`journalctl -u larder-worker -n 50`, and call Tomás in #larder-ops with them.
